  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a mixed-integer gradient-based optimization approach that combines local gradient descent with global heuristic exploration. The key innovation is:\n\n- Using integer variables to represent binary-like decisions for high/low h[i] regions\n- Allowing continuous transitions between modes via penalty relaxation\n- Maintaining feasibility through exact repair steps\n\nThis approach addresses the nonconvexity by explicitly modeling binary decisions while allowing smooth transitions, combining the global perspective of integer programming with the efficiency of gradient methods.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe define:\n\nInteger variables z[i] \u2208 {0,1} representing ideal binary decisions\nContinuous variables y[i] \u2208 [0,1] representing actual h[i] values\nPenalty parameter \u03bc controlling binary enforcement\n\nThe relaxed problem minimizes:\n\nC\u2085 = (2/n) * max_k (y \u22c6 (1 - y))[k] + \u03bc \u03a3|y[i] - z[i]|\n\nSubject to:\n- \u03a3y[i] = n/2\n- 0 \u2264 y[i] \u2264 1\n\nWe solve this using an augmented Lagrangian method, updating y and z iteratively.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na) Initialization\n1. If initial_h_values exists:\n   - Scale to sum=n/2\n   - Initialize y = h_init\n   - Compute z[i] = 1 if y[i] > 0.5 else 0\n2. Else:\n   - Initialize y uniformly: y[i] = 0.5\n   - z[i] = 0\n3. Compute initial C\u2085 and store as best solution\n\nb) Optimization loop\nWhile time remaining > 0:\n1. Compute current C\u2085\n2. Compute gradient for current max lag\n3. Perform gradient descent step on y\n4. Update z via rounding\n5. Apply feasibility repair\n6. Update penalty parameter \u03bc\n7. Track best solution\n\nc) Gradient computation\nFor identified lag k*:\n1. Compute overlap region\n2. Gradient = (2/n) * (1 - y_shifted - y)\n3. Clip to feasible region\n\nd) Repair procedure\n1. Clip y[i] to [0,1]\n2. Rescale to ensure sum(y)=n/2\n3. Update z via exact rounding\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points = 100\n- Initial \u03bc = 0.1\n- \u03bc update factor = 1.2\n- Learning rate = 0.001\n- Max iterations = 5000\n- Random perturbation fraction = 0.01\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nEach iteration: O(n)\n5000 iterations: 5000 * 100 = 5e5 operations\nRobustness via penalty updates and feasibility repair\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Validate 0 \u2264 y[i] \u2264 1\n- Verify sum(y)=n/2\n- Recompute C\u2085 with original evaluator\n- Return (y, C\u2085, n_points)",
